WEBVTT

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:03.710
Previously on Everwood.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:40.310
Need any help?

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:41.930
Whatever happened to instant coffee?

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.170
You get some hot water, you pour it over a mug full of grins, and it's done.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.320
It may have tasted like fossil fuel, but it only took 30 seconds.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:50.840
You're right.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:51.900
We all miss the good old days.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.800
Hey, you'll probably get going.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.320
The service starts in half an hour.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:56.890
You sure you don't mind watching Julian?

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:57.890
That depends.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:00:59.970
What's the going rate on that baby?

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.660
I think you owe me a few freebies, Dad, considering you missed her birth and all.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.100
So Delia really doesn't want to go to the funeral?

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.080
Well, I talked to her, and she seems to want to stay home.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.000
I mean, how many funerals are you supposed to attend before you're 13, you know?

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.650
Hey, sorry, I'm late.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.390
No, you're fine. Jake, almost ready?

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.990
Oh, everything got all messed up at the house.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.410
The inspectors were supposed to come tomorrow, but the other day it's mixed up, and the buyers are on their way now.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:21.050
There you go.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.250
Oh, good man.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:24.630
He feels terrible. He'll get there as soon as possible.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.430
Okay, then. We should head out.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:29.980
Dad, I'll call you out.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.340
the service. Don't worry, we'll be fine. You just go and say goodbye to your friend now.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:59.680
I'm out in town. All right.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.900
Harper, let's see what you got.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:07.090
Good morning.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.220
I would thank you all for coming today,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.350
but I don't imagine there's anyone here

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.820
who didn't come on their own court.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.200
Irv was amongst the rarest of men

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.480
in both his integrity and his warmth,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.610
and those of us who knew him can count ourselves

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.110
amongst the luckiest.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.500
Irv was, above everything else, a storyteller.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.190
so I thought it appropriate to begin today's eulogies

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.610
with my favorite story about Irv.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.610
It begins six years ago, and ironically,

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.990
just a few short months after the untimely death

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.960
of another great man, my father.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.400
Good afternoon, Mr. Harper.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.760
My apologies about the office.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.040
Typically, I would start you in an exam room,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.260
but since my father's passing,

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.760
we have had a full house around the clock,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:03:59.970
being the sole...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.620
doctor in Everwood is an exhausting distinction, I can assure you. So what brings you in today?

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.270
Your mother thought I should come. My mother? Well, used to her frightening the patients

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:17.140
away, not luring them in. I'm not sure if you know this, but Edna and I have become friendly

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:22.360
in the two months since your father passed. My condolences about that. Yes, well, I appreciate

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:28.710
that, Mr. Harper. He will be deeply missed. Why don't you call me Irv? I'm sorry, but I prefer to

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.410
address patients.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.340
Maintaining professional distance is paramount to the task of caring for the towns and firm.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.540
Really, Irv would be so much easier.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.930
You're not really here for a prostate exam, are you?

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.670
No. I think I'm here to give one.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.400
Laugh much, Doctor?

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.820
When I feel so inclined.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.560
And how frequently is that?

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:54.060
Not.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.960
Listen, Irv, I may be from this town, but...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.080
But unlike the average citizen, I am not an idiot.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.780
I have known that you and my mother have been friendly,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.680
as you so delicately put it, for some weeks now.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.780
I assume that you are here to ask me

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.460
about making that relationship public.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:15.720
In a way.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.360
Yes, well, be advised.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:22.790
The individuals of this town are even more narrow-minded

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:23.890
than they are stupid.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.340
They won't appreciate her hurling herself

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.560
into the arms of another man so soon.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.090
compounded with the incidental but very real fact that you are a black dude their

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:45.390
phrasing will be more colorful I'm no stranger to racism doctor and Edna and I

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:50.170
don't really care what other people think we care what you think and with

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:55.590
your blessing I would like to make an honest woman out of her what I'm asking to

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:59.980
marry your mother I'm perfectly aware of what you are asking are you as crazy

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:01.460
Probably.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.080
Did you hear nothing of what I've just said?

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:05.720
Yeah, but I didn't take it very seriously.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:06.440
Well, you should.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:07.740
So should she?

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:08.820
Dear God!

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.230
It is one thing for a grieving widow

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.950
to find temporary comfort in the arms of another man.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:18.870
But marriage?

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.700
Edna said you would probably react that way.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:23.420
She did.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.560
But I thought I would try.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.440
Your approval would mean a lot to her and to me.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:29.960
I'm going to give you some advice.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.260
something even better than my approval I am going to pretend that this

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:44.270
conversation never occurred it was nice chatting with you I'm not sure what the

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:49.780
problem is Harold marriage marriage what does she even know about this man what

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.560
does she know except that he's some vagabond that that she met at a church

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:58.060
picnic it's true he could be a homicidal maniac preying on hungry church-going

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:59.970
women don't get my hopes up

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.950
Well, I admit it is a little hasty, but why don't you just talk to her, Harold?

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.020
Let her know your hesitations, and maybe she'll listen.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.840
You say that as though we're dealing with a rational individual.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.860
Your mother is extremely rational.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:14.640
Where everyone else is concerned, maybe, but not with me.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:17.810
After my conversation with her new beau this afternoon,

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.390
I'll be lucky to keep all of my appendages when next we meet.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:28.960
Hello? Hello, where you are?

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:34.710
Harold Abbott, Jr., you are the best son any woman could ask for.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:36.270
Irv told me everything.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:36.710
He did?

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.090
How understanding you were, how supportive.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:45.250
I have to say, Jr., I thought for sure you were going to act like your usual stiff-shirted, dip-waddy self.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.890
But when you didn't, I was just so happy.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:48.770
Well, actually...

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.750
I loved your pops, Buster. You know that.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:56.170
But I feel like he sent me Irv as his way of saying, keep on living, doll cakes.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.090
And I was so glad you could see that.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:07:59.970
Well, now that you...

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.280
Originally, Harper and I were just going to go to City Hall and get hitched.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.640
But I'm thinking, if my own son can get on board this thing, why not the whole town?

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.680
So I've decided to throw a big, fat ceremony and invite everybody.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.540
And even better, I'd like to do it here.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:15.680
What do you say, Junior?

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:29.880
Hey, I was thinking of starting with the wedding march.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.920
and then segwaying into Ave Maria for the arrival of the bride and the groom.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:34.360
Does that sound good, Doug?

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.080
Do I look as though I care, Thurman?

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.240
You know, we set it for three. Where is everybody?

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:42.660
Oh, I just talked with Brenda Baxworth. Her back is out.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.300
I didn't want to invite her anyway.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:46.380
Edna, I think we can do this without an audience.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:47.580
Oh, we certainly can.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.820
Bright, Amy, hurry up. I'm gonna marry your grandma.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.500
Harper, are you sure the flyer had three on it?

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.540
This is absurd. You know perfectly well why they're not here.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:57.420
It's a boycott.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:58.020
Boycott?

00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:59.970
Yes. You didn't really expect...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.720
anyone to endorse this ridiculous

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.800
arrangement, did you? Harold. I am sorry,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.940
Rose, but enough is enough. I thought you

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.460
were in favor of this shindig.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.040
Why don't you ask your fiancé here

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.980
how that conversation between us really

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.460
went? Did you lie to me, Harper?

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.880
Of course she did. There's no telling

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.940
what number of things the man is lying about, because

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.740
the man is a perfect stranger.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.940
Well, if you felt that way, why didn't you say anything?

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.940
I tried to. You were too busy acting as if

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:26.840
you'd just won the publisher's clearinghouse.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:28.840
Okay, smartass. If the whole town

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:29.900
agrees with you, why?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:30.640
Why is Thurman here?

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.820
Because I'm paying him!

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:32.980
Get out of here, Thurman!

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:33.460
Out!

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:33.860
Now!

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.340
Just stay upstairs, kids!

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:37.440
All right.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:38.140
You want to do this?

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:39.610
Let's do it.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:40.370
By all means.

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:46.320
You're upset because I like my coffee hot and black.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.040
First, ew.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:51.140
Second, that is not what is upsetting to me, mother.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.200
Dad's body isn't even cold and you are behaving like a love-sick teenager.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.580
It is bizarre.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.980
It is disrespectful to his memory and...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.840
painful to those of us who are still grieving don't you dare talk about

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.380
grieving to me I cried my eyes silly over that man yeah I'll bet it was a

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:12.720
tough month what about you you had us back in that office a week after he

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.060
passed I did not make you come back and last I checked people didn't stop

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:20.600
getting sick just because a man dies can't anyone in this family get a word

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:25.700
in age-wise now if you'll both just cool down for a few seconds and do me the

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.700
courtesy of letting me apologize.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:28.880
Apologize?

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:29.380
Yes, Edna.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.100
all of this, and I mean all of this is my fault.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:34.300
It was my idea to get married.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.720
I come from a generation where it's just the honorable thing

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:39.480
to do when you get serious with someone.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:43.190
And I went and lied to your mom about our conversation

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.510
because I couldn't break her heart

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:46.690
just when I'm getting to know it.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.420
Fact is, Harold, you're right.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:53.220
We can't possibly love each other the way two people who

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.140
lived a life together do.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.340
And the timing of this couldn't be worse.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.450
But it's just the way it is.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:05.780
I mean no disrespect to your father.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.400
I'm sorry I won't get a chance to get to know him,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:10.960
because he sure kept great company.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:18.940
Rose, thank you for opening your home to us today.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.340
But if you don't mind, we're going to go take care of this

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:22.880
at City Hall.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.140
Come on, Edna.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:29.390
I don't want you opening your mouth again until you say I do.

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:46.280
Still think I'm crazy I've been wrong many times in my life

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:58.830
I've never been more wrong than in my estimation of Irv and Edna Harper's affection for each other. There's nothing temporary about it

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:05.210
The love that they shared was a living example of God's grace on this earth.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:16.970
As for Irv himself, he called Everwood his home for long enough to change us all, but

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:20.200
still far too short a time.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:29.040
And so today we bid him farewell with heavy hearts, with weary souls.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:37.990
He was a husband, father, grandfather, friend to us all.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:45.930
You'll be missed. May he rest in peace.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:11.360
What's so funny?

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.070
I like this country doctor's character.

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:15.310
He's snappy.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:19.420
Hey, wait a minute. What do you got there?

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:20.000
Let me see that.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:23.140
I thought you were reading Little Women.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:26.550
I was, but I could tell Beth was about to die,

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.390
and frankly, I can't really handle it right now.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:34.200
I like to keep it close.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:35.920
Brown residence.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.060
Brown residence?

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:37.840
That's new.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.300
I'm trying it out.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:40.260
Formal.

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:40.660
A little.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:41.980
Is your grandfather there?

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:43.120
It's for you.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:47.480
Hey, hold it up.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:48.660
I was going to ask you that.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:49.900
Oh, we're just doing fine.

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:51.140
Well, we're heading for the wake.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.290
I'll call you as soon as we're on our way home.

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:54.570
And if you need to reach me, you'll-

00:13:54.570 --> 00:13:55.210
Oh, OK, OK.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:56.310
We'll see you later then, OK?

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:04.140
You hung up on me.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:05.960
Why am I not surprised?

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.140
Hey, you think we should grab something to bring over there?

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.220
Oh, that's a good idea.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:11.620
We could pick up a pie or some flowers or something.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:19.750
You okay?

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:22.640
I just still can't believe it's real.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.640
The whole service kept waiting for someone to see there by their mistake.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:28.320
I know.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:29.820
You see him every day, Andy.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.830
And every day is the same cup of regular in the morning and a decaf in the afternoon.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:37.460
We didn't always have a conversation, but he was just such a part of my life.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.790
Although, I guess I wouldn't have been seeing him every day anymore.

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:44.570
I won't be seeing anyone every day anymore.

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:47.750
Mimi?

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:50.050
Hmm, I'm sorry. I just got a little dizzy.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:51.570
Hmm.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:03.040
You stay here with Ephraim.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:04.540
I'm gonna get you something to eat and get your blood sugar.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.040
Yeah, maybe that'll help.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:28.390
Freaked out about leaving?

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.130
So I was trying not to think about all the goodbyes.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:34.510
I thought I'd be back so often I wouldn't have to.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.870
I mean, when you say goodbye to people you've known your whole life.

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:37.600
Yeah.

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:40.640
And then something like this happens and you realize

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.500
that you have to create some kind of closure when you can

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.040
because there's too many times in life when you don't get the chance

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:48.300
and then you're just left feeling like...

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:49.740
You have a cut that never healed properly

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:51.200
and now you have a scar that will never go away.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:57.140
And that scar is a constant reminder of the things you didn't do when you had a chance.

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:04.610
I didn't hug my mom the day she died.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.270
She tried to, but I was upset or nervous about my recital or something,

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:09.290
so I just pushed her away.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:11.880
My scar.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:15.450
I'm sorry.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:17.310
It's all right. No, it's fine.

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:19.090
It's just now that whenever something like this happens,

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:20.450
that's exactly where my mind goes.

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.190
You know, I start thinking about all the stuff that I didn't do

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:25.150
or that I regret not doing if I knew I'd never have another chance.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:27.700
Anything come to mind today?

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:28.710
Actually, yeah.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:33.880
The only problem is I can't quite tell if the thing I want to do

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:37.420
is just being honest or betraying somebody else's trust.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:39.640
It's a fine line, you know?

